; 01-understanding.scenarios.udon — a day in the life, morning
; BDD scenarios for the future udon path/query tool. Conventions and the
; op vocabulary: ../README.md. Every :path value is a QUOTED STRING in the
; provisional syntax of spec/msc/adjudication-2026-07-paths-and-silences.md
; (Part A) — the journey shapes are the contract here, not the spelling.
; Scenarios carrying the .gap trait document an affordance the current
; packet does NOT cover; each has a |gap child saying what was wanted.

|feature[understanding].day-in-the-life
  :date <2026-07-21>
  :tool udon-path
  :path-syntax provisional-2026-07
  :corpus ../corpus/

  The surveyor arrives in the archema neighborhood knowing nothing. Its
  first hour is pure understanding: orient, address, resolve, follow.
  Nothing is written before noon.

  |scenario[skeleton-first]
    :at <09:05> :agent surveyor
    :file corpus/asf-processes.process-map.udon

    A 170-line file the surveyor has never seen. Reading it whole costs
    context; the skeleton is the orientation move.

    |given the process map as shipped
    |when
      |skeleton :path "|process-map[asf-processes]" :depth 2
    |then the skeleton lists each child element with name, key, traits,
      suffix flags, and a child/prose summary — enough to navigate,
      nothing more
      !:text:
        |process-map[asf-processes]  (5 attrs)
          |meta-meta-process[self-governance]  (2 attrs, prose 4 lines)
          |cluster[spine]      (3 attrs, 3 processes, prose 2 lines)
          |cluster[verification]   (1 attr, 3 processes)
          |cluster[integration]    (1 attr, 3 processes)
          |cluster[coherence]      (1 attr, 2 processes)
          |diagram[core-loop]      (1 attr, raw 6 lines)
          |diagnosis               (prose 4 lines)
          |open-unknowns           (1 attr, prose 3 lines)
      |expect :count 8 :note top-level children under the root
      The `?` suffixes (coherence-stewardship, follow-up-audits,
      empirical-verification) surface in the skeleton — they are identity
      material, not decoration.

  |scenario[exactly-one-read]
    :at <09:12> :agent surveyor
    :file corpus/asf-processes.process-map.udon

    |given the process map as shipped
    |when
      |at :path "|process-map[asf-processes]|cluster[spine]|process[valve]:health"
    |then
      |expect :value under-fed :type string
      `at` returns exactly one value or errors; here the path is fully
      keyed, so it cannot be plural.

  |scenario[ambiguity-fails-loudly]
    :at <09:14> :agent surveyor
    :file corpus/asf-processes.process-map.udon

    The surveyor lazily asks for "the cluster". Four exist. The tool must
    never guess.

    |given the process map as shipped
    |when
      |at :path "|process-map[asf-processes]|cluster"
    |then
      |expect-error :code PathNotUnique :matches 4
      The error payload carries the disambiguating identities
      ([spine] [verification] [integration] [coherence]) so the recovery
      is one edit away — fail-loudly must also mean fail-helpfully.

  |scenario[all-with-trait-filter]
    :at <09:22> :agent ordinator
    :file corpus/terrestris.ordinum.udon

    |given the ordinum as shipped
    |when
      |all :path "||promise.capability"
    |then
      |expect :count 2 :keys [coriolis seeded-asymmetry]
      Traits AND-filter; `||` descends any depth. Promise classes are
      traits, so the class taxonomy is path-visible for free.
    |when
      |all :path "||promise.state"
    |then
      |expect :count 6 :note document order, phase by phase

  |scenario[typed-keys-are-typed]
    :at <09:30> :agent operator-1
    :file corpus/operata-live.workspace.udon

    The workspace keys intents by integer id; one legacy import kept its
    zero-padded STRING id. The path scanner types bracket contents by the
    same value rules as the document side (packet P4).

    |given the workspace as shipped
    |when
      |at :path "||intent[311]:status"
    |then
      |expect :value projected
    |when
      |at :path '||intent["0042"]:status'
    |then
      |expect :value realized
      The path needed single quotes at the UDON layer so the key's double
      quotes survive; carrier syntax and path syntax interact exactly here.
    |when
      |at :path "||intent[42]"
    |then
      |expect-error :code PathNotFound
      Integer 42 matches nothing: "0042" is a string key and typed
      equality does not coerce. Silent empty would have hidden a real
      modeling distinction.

  |scenario[suffix-flags-are-invisible].gap
    :at <09:40> :agent surveyor
    :file corpus/asf-processes.process-map.udon

    The founding consumer's most load-bearing annotation is the `?`
    suffix — "a genuinely unknown sub-process we'd have to design." The
    surveyor wants exactly those.

    |given the process map as shipped
    |when the surveyor looks for a path meaning "every process whose $?
      flag is set"
    |then no syntax expresses it: wildcards select elements, traits
      filter by $traits, but no form filters by any other attribute —
      including the specially-designated $? that the suffix sugar writes
    |workaround
      |all :path "||process"
      ...then filter host-side on the `$?` attribute of each result.
    |gap
      :packet-ref P9
      :shape attribute-existence filter, or suffix-flags as first-class
        path material the way traits already are
      Suffixes and traits are siblings in the identity model ("|el?"
      desugars to an attribute exactly as ".trait" does), yet paths can
      see one and not the other. Six live sites in the real process map.

  |scenario[follow-reference-multiplicity]
    :at <09:52> :agent surveyor
    :file corpus/asf-processes.process-map.udon

    The valve's :fed-by is a STACKED attribute — two reference values,
    one per edge, written as two assignments.

    |given the process map as shipped
    |when
      |at :path "||process[valve]:fed-by"
    |then
      |expect :value @process[finding-routing] :note last assignment wins
        under `at` (packet P2); the stack is the attr's own semantics,
        not path ambiguity
    |when
      |all :path "||process[valve]:fed-by"
    |then
      |expect :count 2 :note both references, source order
    |when
      |at :path "||process[valve]:fed-by@:health"
    |then
      |expect-error :code ReferencePlural
      Following the stack under `at` errors on plural (packet P6 —
      multiplicity inherits, it is not re-legislated).
    |when
      |all :path "||process[valve]:fed-by@:health"
    |then
      |expect :values [broken healthy]
      `all` fans out through both referenced definitions.

  |scenario[proto-path-in-the-wild].gap
    :at <10:05> :agent ordinator
    :file corpus/terrestris.ordinum.udon

    The ordinum's defeasances carry quoted qualified ids — :voids
    "2.water-covered-surface" — a hand-rolled path convention that
    predates any path tool. Resolving one is a two-hop journey.

    |given the ordinum as shipped
    |when the ordinator resolves the defeasance in phase[abyssal] that
      voids "2.water-covered-surface"
    |then the "2." qualifier means "the phase whose :num is 2" — an
      attribute-VALUE lookup no path form expresses; and the promise slug
      is reachable directly because (element-type, key) is document-unique
      anyway
    |workaround
      |all :path "||phase"
      ...host-side, keep the one whose :num reads 2; then
      |at :path "||phase[protogenic]|promises|promise[water-covered-surface]"
    |gap
      :packet-ref P9
      :shape value-equality filter (":num=2"-shaped), or a blessed idiom
        for domain-level foreign keys
      Real consumer files already contain proto-paths as quoted strings
      (ordinum defeasances; lexicon |rel :to slugs; decision-log |ref
      values). The path tool will be asked to resolve conventions it did
      not define. Deciding the posture — resolve, assist, or refuse — is
      a design call the packet does not yet make.

  |scenario[prose-is-half-the-document].gap
    :at <10:20> :agent surveyor
    :file corpus/asf-processes.process-map.udon

    The diagnosis element IS its prose; the conventions live in comments.
    Understanding means reading both.

    |given the process map as shipped
    |when
      |at :path "|process-map[asf-processes]|diagnosis"
    |then
      |expect :node diagnosis
      The element is addressable; its prose arrives as the node's text
      children via the API — no path points AT a paragraph (packet P7,
      deferred, and this morning agrees with the deferral so far)
    |when the surveyor asks for every comment in the file (the
      conventions block, the section banners)
    |then comments are events the parser preserves, but paths cannot
      address them at all
    |gap
      :packet-ref P7
      :shape none yet — API-side access on the parent node was enough
        for every read this morning; the pressure P7 predicts is real
        but it lands on the PATCH side (03-modifying, fix-typo) before
        the read side

  |scenario[cross-document-seam]
    :at <10:40> :agent surveyor
    :file corpus/archema.concept-matrix.udon

    |given the concept matrix as shipped
    |when
      |at :path "||concept[decision-authority]|carrier"
    |then
      |expect-error :code PathNotUnique :matches 2
      The two carriers are keyless siblings, distinguishable only by
      their :in attribute — which paths cannot filter on (P9's absence
      again; keying every carrier just to be addressable would duplicate
      the :in data into the identity)
    |when the surveyor follows a carrier: :in names a FILE, :ref holds the
      in-document reference
    |then resolution is a host journey — open the :in document, evaluate
      the :ref there. The path language is document-scoped and the packet
      is silent on whether that is a boundary or a v1 scope choice; this
      corpus votes "boundary, but say so out loud" — the two-attr idiom
      (:in + :ref) worked fine and kept each file's reference space clean.
